        <PAGEOBJECT XPOS="115.0967" YPOS="155.32010393701" OwnPage="0" ItemID="241163088" PTYPE="4" WIDTH="283.9041" HEIGHT="66.6046960629899" FRTYPE="0" CLIPEDIT="1" PWIDTH="1" PLINEART="1" LOCALSCX="1" LOCALSCY="1" LOCALX="0" LOCALY="0" LOCALROT="0" PICART="1" SCALETYPE="1" RATIO="1" COLUMNS="1" COLGAP="0" AUTOTEXT="0" EXTRA="0" TEXTRA="0" BEXTRA="0" REXTRA="0" VAlign="0" FLOP="1" PLTSHOW="0" BASEOF="0" textPathType="0" textPathFlipped="0" path="M0 0 L283.904 0 L283.904 66.6047 L0 66.6047 L0 0 Z" copath="M0 0 L283.907 0 L283.907 66.6048 L0 66.6048 L0 0 Z" gXpos="115.0967" gYpos="155.32010393701" gWidth="282.55" gHeight="45.4146798798563" ALIGN="0" LAYER="0" NEXTITEM="-1" BACKITEM="-1">
            <StoryText>
                <DefaultStyle ALIGN="0" LINESP="28" FONT="Special Elite Regular" FONTSIZE="33" SCALEH="88" KERN="-9"/>
                <ITEXT CH="Pittsburgh's Newest"/>
                <para ALIGN="1"/>
                <ITEXT CH="Invasive Species"/>
                <trail ALIGN="1"/>
            </StoryText>
        </PAGEOBJECT>
        <PAGEOBJECT XPOS="115.660910526316" YPOS="219.208" OwnPage="0" ItemID="241822368" PTYPE="4" WIDTH="281.243089473684" HEIGHT="272.2848" FRTYPE="0" CLIPEDIT="0" PWIDTH="1" PLINEART="1" LOCALSCX="1" LOCALSCY="1" LOCALX="0" LOCALY="0" LOCALROT="0" PICART="1" SCALETYPE="1" RATIO="1" COLUMNS="2" COLGAP="7.2" AUTOTEXT="0" EXTRA="0" TEXTRA="0" BEXTRA="0" REXTRA="0" VAlign="0" FLOP="1" PLTSHOW="0" BASEOF="0" textPathType="0" textPathFlipped="0" path="M0 0 L281.243 0 L281.243 272.285 L0 272.285 L0 0 Z" copath="M0 0 L281.243 0 L281.243 272.285 L0 272.285 L0 0 Z" gXpos="115.660910526316" gYpos="219.207999999987" gWidth="0" gHeight="0" PSTYLE="Default Paragraph Style" LAYER="0" NEXTITEM="-1" BACKITEM="-1">
            <StoryText>
                <DefaultStyle PARENT="Default Paragraph Style" LINESP="10" FONT="Bell MT Regular" FONTSIZE="9" KERN="0" LANGUAGE="en_US"/>
                <ITEXT FONT="Bell MT Bold" FONTSIZE="9" CH="Tali Kirschenbaum"/>
                <para PARENT="Default Paragraph Style" ALIGN="1" LINESP="10"/>
                <ITEXT FONT="Bell MT Italic" FONTSIZE="9" KERN="0" CH="Not an Ecologist "/>
                <para PARENT="Default Paragraph Style" ALIGN="1" LINESP="10"/>
                <para/>
                <ITEXT CH="    You’ve seen them around. They didn’t exist here a few years ago, but now they’re all over the place. If you don’t know what they are, you might think they look kinda cool. Really, they’re destroying our environment."/>
                <para/>
                <ITEXT CH="    Pittsburgh’s newest invasive species has no natural predators. It destroys its competition, becoming all you see. It behaves unpredictably, darting around or stopping at the slightest disturbance. It sees in all directions. It tries to avoid people yet feeds on the systems we rely on. It flies around the city, destroying natural resources as it goes. It is not beholden to human control. Worst  of all, it knows where you live."/>
                <para/>
                <ITEXT CH="    Pittsburgh isn’t the first city overtaken by this pest, but it offers ideal conditions that enable the species to thrive. Known to most as the Waymo—and to science as Vehicula autonoma—this creature upends the natural order, bringing chaos to that which is familiar and threatening an ecosystem that has developed over generations."/>
                <para/>
                <ITEXT CH="    The Waymo disturbs the typical flow of nature by waiting to turn left until all oncoming traffic has passed. The Waymo disrupts the food chain by preying on the formerly-invasive Uber population, which previously drove the native taxicab nearly to extinction. The Waymo claims space in the roads, leaving native cars and bicycles to sit idle until, eventually, they die out."/>
                <para/>
                <ITEXT CH="    Most invasive species are eradicated by forced removal. This one is different. The only way to defeat the Waymo is to refuse all contact with it. Do not approach it. Don’t even look at it. It is watching you. It subsists on your intrigue. Do not feed it. It is a dangerous animal, but if you feel inclined to truly inconvenience a Waymo, there is one simple solution: jaywalk in front of it."/>
                <para/>
                <para/>
                <para/>
                <para/>
            </StoryText>
        </PAGEOBJECT>
        <PAGEOBJECT XPOS="406.5008" YPOS="390.7" OwnPage="0" ItemID="241825200" PTYPE="2" WIDTH="294.0214" HEIGHT="276.8016" FRTYPE="0" CLIPEDIT="1" PWIDTH="1" PCOLOR2="Black" PLINEART="1" LOCALSCX="1" LOCALSCY="1" LOCALX="-788.915999999999" LOCALY="-263.202" LOCALROT="0" PICART="1" SCALETYPE="1" RATIO="1" PRFILE="sRGB display profile (ICC v2.2)" IRENDER="0" path="M0 0 L294.021 0 L294.021 276.802 L0 276.802 L0 0 Z" copath="M0 0 L294.019 0 L294.019 276.804 L0 276.804 L0 0 Z" ImageRes="2" gXpos="406.5008" gYpos="1222.7032" gWidth="306.5422" gHeight="302.8768" GRExtM="3" GRTYPM="2" GRSTARTXM="0" GRSTARTYM="0" GRENDXM="363.548833403386" GRENDYM="0" GRFOCALXM="0" GRFOCALYM="0" GRSCALEM="1" GRSKEWM="0" LAYER="0" NEXTITEM="-1" BACKITEM="-1">
